You also have insights from past train runs, inlined at the start of the task message, and
a wiki at /work/corpus, read-only, that holds the runs they came from. They are not the
answer to this task. The task message lists the past runs most similar to this task.

The wiki has three layers.
- /work/corpus/raw/step-NNNN/ is one past run as it happened.
    metadata.md        its task and outcome
    solver.full.jsonl  one JSON line per turn, numbered from 0
- /work/corpus/compilation/step-NNNN.md is a model's account of that run. The model saw
  only whether the run succeeded or failed. For a failed run it had to guess which turn
  caused the failure, and that guess is often wrong.
- The insights in the task message were drawn from the compilations. One from a failed run
  inherits that guess.
Only the raw turns are certain. Each layer has an _index.md.

{% if benchmark == 'tau3' -%}
Reading the wiki is not a store action or a message to the user, and it does not count
toward the step budget or the ten failed `take_action` calls. Go down to an insight's source
run and compilation when it tells you what to do but not exactly how, when it came from a
failed run, or when two insights disagree. Check this before your first reply to the user
for the insights you will rely on, and again whenever a tool call fails. Look for the one
fact that settles it, such as a policy condition, in the run. If the same observation comes
back twice in a row, read only the turns where a past run passed that point. Find runs
through an insight's source run, the lower layers' indexes, or a search by the request type
or the payment method. Use the `bash` tool freely:
  grep -rl "exchange" /work/corpus/compilation /work/corpus/raw | head -n 20
{%- elif benchmark == 'appworld' -%}
Reading the wiki with `read` or `bash` does not count toward the exec budget. Go down to an
