{%- else -%}
Reading the wiki with `read` or `bash` costs no game steps. Go down to an insight's source run and compilation when it tells you what to do but not exactly how, when it came from a failed run, or when two insights disagree. Check this before your first {{ s.first_action }} for the insights you will rely on, and again whenever an action fails. Look for the one fact that settles it, such as {{ s.fact_example }}, in the run. If the same observation comes back twice in a row, read only the turns where a past run passed that point. Find runs through an insight's source run, the lower layers' indexes, or a search by {{ s.keyword_hint }}. Use the `bash` tool freely:
  grep -rl "{{ s.keyword_example }}" /work/corpus/compilation /work/corpus/raw | head -n 20
{%- endif %}
  for s in 0002 0017; do cat /work/corpus/compilation/step-$s.md; done
  /work/tools/actions step-0017      # every turn's action, one line each
  /work/tools/turn step-0017 7 9     # turns 7 to 9 in full: reasoning, action, observation

Memory rules
- Never replay a past run. It started from a different state than this task, even when
  its task reads the same, and steps that worked there fail here.
- Take from a run only a fact you can check against this task, such as {{ s.fact_example }}.
  Name what differs before you use it.
- From a failed run, take only the action to avoid, never the plan.
